# auditd rules for the Fabric host connector (spec 030).
#
# Load with: auditctl -R fabric.rules
#   or place in /etc/audit/rules.d/fabric.rules and augenrules --load
#
# Native 64-bit x86_64 or arm64 ABI only. The receiver does not decode
# 32-bit compatibility ABIs; do not add b32 rules as if those were covered.
# Review native syscall availability and scope on the target before loading.
#
# Every rule is tagged -k fabric so the receiver (rule_key: fabric) consumes
# only these events and ignores unrelated audit volume on shared hosts.

# Process lifecycle: every execve/execveat — catches ssh, curl | sh, scp,
# uninstrumented tool spawns, and shell escapes.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -S execveat -k fabric

# Outbound network connections: connect, accept, bind — the "agent ssh'd into
# another server" and exfiltration-relevant syscalls.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S connect -S accept -S bind -k fabric

# File access (HIGH VOLUME — enable only with scoping). Uncomment to capture
# openat for a scoped path set instead of system-wide:
# -a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S openat -F dir=/etc -k fabric
# -a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S openat -F dir=/root -k fabric

# Scoping to one agent workload (preferred on shared hosts):
#   -F auid=<loginuid>  limits to a session identity
#   -F exe=<path>       limits to one binary
#   For container-scoped filtering use the eBPF emitter (spec 031) — auditd
#   cannot filter by cgroup.
#
# Lock the ruleset so it cannot be cleared without reboot (last line, once
# stable):
# -e 2
